    <mj-spacer height="10px" />
    <mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;" color="#000000">
  <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 400; margin-top: 2px !important;">
    Rising breast cancer burden
  </h2>
</mj-text>
<mj-image
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.presidentialsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="Rising breast cancer burden"
  href="https://www.presidentialsummary.com/"
/>
<mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5"><strong>What’s happening? </strong>Breast cancer remains the most common cancer among women worldwide, with <a href="https://edition.cnn.com/2026/03/02/health/breast-cancer-rates-increasing-wellness" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none;
                border-bottom: 2px solid #4d3060;
                color: black;">annual cases expected to reach over 3.5Mn</a> by 2050. Similarly, yearly deaths from the disease are projected to surge to 1.4Mn. In 2023, 2.3Mn breast cancers were diagnosed worldwide (1.67Mn cases occurring in high- and upper-middle-income countries) and 764,000 resulting deaths (300,000 deaths occurring in low- and lower-middle-income countries).</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5"><strong>Cause: </strong>Rising obesity and shifts in reproductive patterns (earlier puberty, delayed childbirth, and later menopause) are reshaping breast cancer risk. Lifestyle and behavioral factors accounted for 28% of the global disease burden in 2023. High red meat intake accounts for 11%, followed by tobacco use, high blood sugar, high BMI, alcohol consumption, and physical inactivity. The burden is increasingly shifting to lower-income countries with limited screening, later diagnoses, and higher mortality.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5"><strong>Prevention:</strong> Maintaining a healthy lifestyle, including not smoking, getting sufficient physical activity, lowering red meat consumption, and having a healthy weight, may prevent over a quarter of healthy years lost to illness. The US Preventive Services Task Force recommends mammograms every two years for women aged 40-74. Those with higher risk, such as a family history of breast cancer or obesity, should consult a primary care provider.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5"><strong><em>Fact:</em></strong><em> Breast cancer accounted for around one-in-four cancer diagnoses in 2023.</em></p>
</mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
    <mj-spacer height="10px" />
    <mj-section
